a) Recommended System Requirements
-------------------------------------------------------------
*Multiple Core Processor (or multiple processors).
*As much memory as you can spare (a full 2K frame requires around 12MB of RAM to store and play)
*A good, solid, recent 3D video card (GeForce 6 and newer recommended for use of the FXs features)
*Fast access to frame storage (gigabit ethernet is nice).
*At least a 1024x768 display resolution. 

JefeCheck can run in almost any system, but it remains a high performance program that requires a fair amount of processing power to run correctly, particularly you need a recent 3D accelerator video card. If you whish to use the FXs feature you need a graphics card with a fair amunt of Pixel/Vertex Shader Units. At the time of writing, the latest GeForce 8 video cards have 128 unified Stream Processors, but having 24 is usually enough to run a few FXs on a full 2K frame in real time. Remember that the amount of video RAM is not the only thing that makes a video card powerful, shader units (or stream processors) and memory bandwith are much more important. 


b) How to Install JefeCheck for Linux
-----------------------------------------------------
JefeCheck requires root user access to be installed, as some shared libraries need to be installed into the /usr/local/lib/ folder

1. As a normal user, run the script setup.sh
   >sh setup.sh

   this will create a .JefeCorp folder in your home directory and install all the FXs, LUTs and user manual into it.

2. If you don't already have them in your system, download and install the following shared libraries into /usr/local/lib or /usr/lib or any other place where they can be found by executable programs. The libraries are also included in the installation package in the lib folder

	a. GFL SDK (libgfl.so and libgfle.so) from 
	http://perso.orange.fr/pierre.g/xnview/endownloadgfl.html

3. If you wish (and we recomend it), copy the bin/jefecheck executable to your /usr/local/bin or wherever you store the binaries in your system. This way you can simply call jefecheck from a terminal to start the program. 

If the program fails to start, check that you have all the required libraries installed in your system by typing 
> ldd jefecheck 
or 
>ldd jefecheck_demo

This will list all the required libraries that should be installed on your system.

If you have absolutely no idea what a shared library is or how to install it, please ask your system administrator or someone who can help you.

d) Getting Help
--------------------------------------------------------
To get help please visit 
http://jefecheck.jefecorp.com/support.html

You can access the support forums, download the User Manual, Quick Reference Guide and Video Tutorials, submit bug reports and feature requests etc.

You can also write to support@jefecheck.com if you have some urgent question that you cant resolve by reading the manual or asking in the forums.

